  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nMid-level ridging will shift eastward this weekend as a series of \nmid-level lows traverse the western CONUS next week. However, these \nsystems are expected to remain well north of Texas, with low \nconfidence in any associated frontal boundaries reaching the region. \nA weak coastal trough may develop Tuesday, followed by a general \nweakness in the surface pressure pattern through late week. \n\nIncreasing moisture and weak disturbances will support moderate rain \nchances Monday night into Tuesday, followed by lower chances Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday. Rain chances increase again Thursday \nafternoon, becoming moderate, with low to moderate chances \ncontinuing through Friday evening. \n\nTemperatures will trend slightly cooler as the ridge shifts east, \nwith highs ranging from the lower 80s to around 90 Saturday, then \ngenerally 80-85 degrees next week. Overnight lows will remain in the \n60s to near 70. \n\nP-ETSS guidance suggests increasing potential for minor coastal \nflooding late this weekend into early next week. Tidal levels near \n1.6 ft MSL Saturday are forecast to rise, potentially exceeding 2.0 \nft MSL by Monday. Increasing swell periods from around 5 seconds \nSaturday to 8 seconds Monday may further enhance coastal impacts. A \nmoderate risk of rip currents will continue through at least Sunday \ndue to elevated onshore flow and astronomical tides.",
